@database "log_viewer"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: log_viewer.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Log Viewer"
@{h1}Log Viewer

The application writes what it does into log files: the API server, the
command line, profiling and security each into their own. This plugin
reads them. You see the newest entries in the @{b}Logs@{ub} panel, narrow
them by level and search, and follow them while they grow; an agent gets
three tools to list the files, read their last lines and search them --
for example a coder agent looking for the traceback behind a failed call.
Only the files named in the configuration are ever opened, by the panel
and by the tools alike.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Logs panel with the newest entries of a log file"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Logs panel  " link panel}

For agents and developers
  @{"  log_viewer_list  " link list}
  @{"  log_viewer_tail  " link tail}
  @{"  log_viewer_search  " link search}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Logs panel"
@{h1}The Logs panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Debugging@{ub}, "Logs") or the command
palette. It belongs to no session: it shows the log files of the server,
whichever chat is open.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Logs panel"}

@{h2}What you see

@{bullet}The @{b}file@{ub} menu lists the configured log files that exist.
A file's rotations -- @{tt}api.log.1@{utt}, @{tt}api.log.2@{utt} and so
on, up to the first number missing -- belong to it: the panel reads
through them as if they were one file.
@{bullet}Next to the filters, how many entries are shown and the size of
the file, with its rotations "in 3 files".
@{bullet}The entries, oldest at the top, each with its time (the full
date and time as a tooltip), its level and its message. Errors and
critical entries are tinted.

An entry is a line that starts with a date and time, together with the
lines after it that do not: a traceback folds under its first line and
opens with a click; entries you opened stay open when the list reloads.
The panel knows these line forms:

@{code}
2026-01-01 10:00:00,000 INFO agent_system.api Server started
2026-01-01 10:00:00 INFO [agent_system.profiling] Profiling started
2026-01-01 10:00:00,000 - agent_system.api - WARNING - Slow answer
2026-01-01 10:00:00 | ERROR | DENIED | GET /admin | user=bob
@{body}

@{tt}WARN@{utt} counts as warning, @{tt}FATAL@{utt} as critical. A line
with a time but no level is an entry without a level; lines at the top of
a file that no timed line leads form an entry of their own, without time
and level. The panel's own requests in an access log are left out while
they are routine (debug, info or no level); a warning or error about them
is shown.

@{h2}What you can do

@{table}
Control | Does
Search | shows only entries that contain the text, anywhere in the entry, its traceback included; upper and lower case alike, no patterns
Last 100 ... 5000 | how many of the newest matching entries are shown; 500 at the start
Error, Warning, Info, Debug | which levels are shown; Error takes critical along. With all four on, entries without a level are shown too; with some off, they are not
Follow | on (the start): the newest entry stays in view. Off: a reload keeps your place
@{body}

A different file, search, count or level always starts at the newest
entry. The toolbar reloads at once, or every 5 seconds when you switch the
automatic reload on (it is off the first time; the menu next to it sets
another interval), and says when the entries were last loaded. File,
filters, Follow and the reload setting are kept in the browser for the
next time.

@{h2}When there is nothing to show

@{bullet}@{b}No log files@{ub}: none of the configured files exists yet.
@{bullet}@{b}No level chosen@{ub}: all four level buttons are off; the
server is not asked.
@{bullet}@{b}No entries@{ub}: the file has nothing to show -- it is empty
or holds only blank lines and the panel's own polling, which are left out
-- or nothing matches the search and levels. The panel says which.
@{bullet}@{b}The log could not be loaded@{ub}, with the server's reason,
when a call fails -- for a file that disappeared or cannot be read.

None of them keeps the entries shown before, so nothing old passes for
current.

@{h2}The two calls behind it

@{table}
Call | Answers
GET /plugins/log_viewer/logs/list | every configured file with exists and rotation_count; one that exists also with size and modified across its rotations and rotation_files
GET /plugins/log_viewer/logs/content/<name> | entries, each with timestamp, level and message, oldest first
@{body}

The content call takes @{tt}lines@{utt} (1 to 5000, default 500),
@{tt}levels@{utt} (comma-separated from debug, info, warning, error,
critical; empty for all, entries without a level included) and
@{tt}search@{utt}. It answers 404 for a name that is not configured --
whatever else a path names is never opened -- or a configured file that
does not exist, 422 for a count out of range or an unknown level, and 500
with the reason when the file cannot be read. It reads the files from
their end and stops as soon as it has the entries asked for, so a long log
is not read whole.
@endnode

@node list "log_viewer_list"
@{h1}log_viewer_list

Lists the configured log files, so an agent knows which names the other
two tools take. It has no parameters.

@{h2}Answer

@{tt}logs@{utt}: one entry per configured file, in the configured order,
with @{tt}name@{utt} (the name to pass on) and @{tt}exists@{utt}; one that
exists also with @{tt}size@{utt} in bytes and @{tt}modified@{utt} as a
Unix time. Rotations are not listed, and the size is that of the file
itself.

@{code json}
{"logs": [{"name": "logs/api.log", "size": 48213, "modified": 1790000000.0, "exists": true},
          {"name": "logs/security.log", "exists": false}]}
@{body}
@endnode

@node tail "log_viewer_tail"
@{h1}log_viewer_tail

The newest lines of one log file -- what happened last.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
log_file | required; a configured name, exactly as log_viewer_list gives it (logs/api.log, not logs\api.log or an absolute path)
lines | how many of the newest lines; a whole number of at least 1, 500 when left out
@{body}

@{h2}Answer

@{tt}log_file@{utt}, @{tt}lines@{utt} (the lines as text, oldest first),
@{tt}total_lines@{utt} (all lines of the file) and
@{tt}returned_lines@{utt}. Lines are lines here, not entries: a traceback
is several of them. The tool reads the file itself, not its rotations.

A refusal is @{tt}{"error": "..."}@{utt}:

@{bullet}"Log file logs/other.log not in allowed list" -- not configured;
@{bullet}"Log file logs/security.log not found" -- configured, not there;
@{bullet}"lines must be a whole number of at least 1, not 0";
@{bullet}"Missing required parameter: log_file";
@{bullet}"Failed to read log file: ..." when it cannot be read.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"log_file": "logs/api.log", "lines": 50}
@{body}
@endnode

@node search "log_viewer_search"
@{h1}log_viewer_search

Finds lines by a regular expression, in one log file or in all of them.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
pattern | required; a Python regular expression, matched against each line on its own, upper and lower case alike
log_file | one configured name; left out, every configured file that exists is searched, in the configured order
max_results | stop after this many matching lines; a whole number of at least 1, 100 when left out
@{body}

@{h2}Answer

@{bullet}@{tt}results@{utt}: the matching lines, each with
@{tt}file@{utt}, @{tt}line_number@{utt} (from 1), @{tt}line@{utt} and
@{tt}match: true@{utt}. A file that cannot be read shows up as
@{tt}{"file": ..., "error": "Failed to read file: ..."}@{utt} and the
search goes on with the next.
@{bullet}@{tt}total_matches@{utt}: the matching lines found.
@{bullet}@{tt}files_searched@{utt}: the files really read -- after
@{tt}max_results@{utt} the rest is not opened.
@{bullet}@{tt}truncated@{utt}: true when the search stopped at
@{tt}max_results@{utt}; there may be more, or exactly that many.
@{bullet}@{tt}pattern@{utt}: the pattern searched.

The lines come in file order, oldest first, so a small
@{tt}max_results@{utt} finds the oldest matches, not the newest; for the
latest error, tail the file instead. A refusal is @{tt}{"error": "..."}@{utt}: "Invalid
regex pattern: ...", "Log file ... not in allowed list", "Log file ... not
found", "max_results must be a whole number of at least 1, not 0",
"Missing required parameter: pattern".

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"pattern": "Traceback|ERROR", "log_file": "logs/api.log", "max_results": 20}
@{body}
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The three tools are named after the plugin instance
(@{tt}log_viewer_list@{utt}, @{tt}log_viewer_tail@{utt},
@{tt}log_viewer_search@{utt} in the default setup); their descriptions and
every parameter's description come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. The names of the files are not in the
descriptions: an agent calls @{tt}log_viewer_list@{utt} first, or is told
them. A refused call answers with an @{tt}error@{utt} text, as listed with
each tool. In the chat, the call's status line ends with the result: "4
log file(s)", "50/12840 lines -- logs/api.log", "3 matches in 2 file(s) --
'Traceback'", or the error.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

Tool answers are ordinary tool results: appended, nothing earlier changes;
the plugin has no hook. What costs is their size: a tail without
@{tt}lines@{utt} returns 500 whole lines, and neither tool cuts a long
line. A small @{tt}lines@{utt}, a narrow pattern and a small
@{tt}max_results@{utt} keep an answer short.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}The tools read a file without its rotations; the panel reads
both.
@{bullet}The tools search line by line with a regular expression; the
panel searches whole entries for plain text.
@{bullet}Tail reads the whole file to count its lines; on a very large
log that takes a moment.
@{bullet}Whatever the logs hold, the agent reads: request paths, user
names, error texts. Configure only files an agent may see.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    log_viewer:
      type: log_viewer
      enabled: true
      log_files:
        - logs/api.log
        - logs/cli.log
        - logs/profiling.log
        - logs/security.log
@{body}

An agent sees the tools when its allowlist names them:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+log_viewer/*"
@{body}

The panel needs nothing more; it is there as soon as the plugin is on.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
log_files | the four files above | the only files the tools and the panel open
@{body}

The names are relative to the working directory the server was started
in, and they are compared as written: a tool call or panel request must
use the name exactly as configured. The plugin keeps no data of its own;
the panel's choices live in the browser.
@endnode
